WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.040 --> 00:00:06.480
Back in 2010, when director Lee Unkrich first
pitched his idea to make Coco, it was going

00:00:06.480 --> 00:00:10.260
to be a completely different movie than the
one we know and love today.

00:00:10.260 --> 00:00:14.210
Yippee-ki-yay, movie lovers, it's Jan here,
and in this video I'm going to reveal some

00:00:14.210 --> 00:00:18.290
of the dramatic changes that Pixar made to
its nineteenth feature film during the course

00:00:18.290 --> 00:00:19.910
of its development.

00:00:19.910 --> 00:00:24.220
By the way, I'm running a giveaway for this
gorgeous Art of Coco hardback book filled

00:00:24.220 --> 00:00:27.640
with stunning concept art for all my subscribers.

00:00:27.640 --> 00:00:31.570
To enter, leave a comment below with your
thoughts about the Coco movie we never got

00:00:31.570 --> 00:00:34.970
to see and make sure you're subscribed to
my channel.

00:00:34.970 --> 00:00:39.140
Quick warning: I will be talking spoilers
for the movie, so take care if you've not

00:00:39.140 --> 00:00:40.840
seen it yet.

00:00:40.840 --> 00:00:44.730
Originally, Coco was going to be a full-on,
traditional musical, which would have been

00:00:44.730 --> 00:00:46.200
a first for Pixar.

00:00:46.200 --> 00:00:51.780
Yes, the film was going to feature six full-length
songs, including several show-stopping song-and-dance

00:00:51.780 --> 00:00:56.500
numbers, all written by Robert Lopez and Kristen
Anderson-Lopez, the husband-and-wife song-writing

00:00:56.500 --> 00:00:58.440
team behind Disney's Frozen.

00:00:58.440 --> 00:01:03.280
Coco's creators even toyed with the idea of
naming the film "Remember Me" after Ernesto

00:01:03.280 --> 00:01:07.150
de la Cruz's biggest hit, which was the first
song composed.

00:01:07.150 --> 00:01:12.570
Indeed, speaking on the Q&amp;A podcast with Jeff
Goldsmith, Coco's screenwriters revealed that,

00:01:12.570 --> 00:01:17.320
in early versions of the movie, Miguel's ancestors
in the Land of the Dead were all cursed so

00:01:17.320 --> 00:01:22.110
they couldn't talk, but could only communicate
by singing, which they all hated because,

00:01:22.110 --> 00:01:27.940
as we know only too well from the final film,
the Rivera family absolutely hates music!

00:01:27.940 --> 00:01:32.560
The filmmakers worked on this full-on musical
version of Coco for several years before eventually

00:01:32.560 --> 00:01:36.420
abandoning that idea as they felt it just
wasn't working.

00:01:36.420 --> 00:01:40.810
That's when they took inspiration from films
such as "Once" and "O Brother, Where Art Thou?"

00:01:40.810 --> 00:01:45.060
and, instead of having the characters in Coco
burst into song about their feelings, they

00:01:45.060 --> 00:01:49.890
decided to only include music that was driven
by actual performances, for example, when

00:01:49.890 --> 00:01:54.500
Miguel sings on stage in the Land of the Dead
or when Héctor sings to Chicharrón, which

00:01:54.500 --> 00:01:58.070
was a scene that co-writer and co-director
Adrian Molina came up with.

00:01:58.070 --> 00:02:02.490
For the team behind Coco, this new approach
felt truer to the kind of tale they wanted

00:02:02.490 --> 00:02:07.300
to tell and it also meant they could showcase
more Mexican-influenced music and make more

00:02:07.300 --> 00:02:11.039
use of the extensive research they'd carried
out for the film.

00:02:11.039 --> 00:02:15.299
On top of that, it also gave the filmmakers
more time to concentrate on the emotional

00:02:15.299 --> 00:02:17.379
side of the story they were telling.

00:02:17.379 --> 00:02:22.139
However, just because Coco changed from being
a full-on musical, doesn't mean that everything

00:02:22.139 --> 00:02:25.299
the filmmakers worked on in that initial version
was thrown out.

00:02:25.299 --> 00:02:30.340
For example, the papel picado sequence which
opens the final film actually started life

00:02:30.340 --> 00:02:34.430
as part of a musical number in the middle
of the movie.

00:02:34.430 --> 00:02:38.480
Speaking of Coco's opening scenes, that part
of the film changed several times over the

00:02:38.480 --> 00:02:39.980
course of the film's development.

00:02:39.980 --> 00:02:44.819
Initially, the movie was going to begin with
a full-on musical number called 'Día de los

00:02:44.819 --> 00:02:48.769
Muertos', featuring performers on stage in
1950s Mexico City.

00:02:48.769 --> 00:02:52.659
The filmmakers thought the song-and-dance
routine would help introduce the idea of the

00:02:52.659 --> 00:02:56.739
Day of the Dead, especially to viewers who
hadn't grown up with that particular tradition.

00:02:56.739 --> 00:03:01.829
So, the deleted opening scene includes important
items for the holiday, for example, the ofrenda,

00:03:01.829 --> 00:03:07.870
an altar set up to honour a person's ancestors;
foods such as calaveras or sugar skulls, pan

00:03:07.870 --> 00:03:13.159
de muerto or sweet rolls, and mole sauce;
and also marigolds, which you can see throughout

00:03:13.159 --> 00:03:15.189
the performance.

00:03:15.189 --> 00:03:19.059
This deleted scene was actually the first
scene that Adrian Molina story-boarded on

00:03:19.059 --> 00:03:20.059
the movie.

00:03:20.059 --> 00:03:24.180
And there's much to enjoy about it, including
the catchy tune which emphasises the celebratory

00:03:24.180 --> 00:03:28.080
nature of the Day of the Dead, and there's
lots of clever little touches too like the

00:03:28.080 --> 00:03:32.519
marigold design of the parasols carried by
the female dancers, and the way the altar

00:03:32.519 --> 00:03:35.629
transforms into a skull and finally a full
skeleton.

00:03:35.629 --> 00:03:38.620
"Día de los Muertos!

00:03:38.620 --> 00:03:41.609
Día de los Muertos!

00:03:41.609 --> 00:03:44.590
Día de los Muertos!"

00:03:44.590 --> 00:03:50.569
[Music]
"The party just goes on and on.

00:03:50.569 --> 00:03:55.799
On and on and on and on!

00:03:55.799 --> 00:03:58.040
On and on!"

00:03:58.040 --> 00:03:59.040
"On!"

00:03:59.040 --> 00:04:00.040
"Día!"

00:04:00.040 --> 00:04:01.040
"De los!"

00:04:01.040 --> 00:04:02.040
"Muertos!

00:04:02.040 --> 00:04:03.040
Muertos!

00:04:03.040 --> 00:04:04.040
Muertos!"

00:04:04.040 --> 00:04:05.040
"Ya!"

00:04:05.040 --> 00:04:08.010
In the end, the filmmakers decided to cut
the musical opening as, instead, they wanted

00:04:08.010 --> 00:04:12.329
us to meet the movie's main character Miguel
straight away so we could learn all about

00:04:12.329 --> 00:04:16.380
him and his family and their polar opposite
feelings about music.

00:04:16.380 --> 00:04:21.150
And lots more songs ended up getting the chop
after the directors decided against making

00:04:21.150 --> 00:04:22.580
a Disney-style musical.

00:04:22.580 --> 00:04:27.320
One of those songs was from the film's first
act and was called 'Way of the Riveras'.

00:04:27.320 --> 00:04:31.770
Basically, Miguel's family sings to him about
what it means to be a member of the Rivera

00:04:31.770 --> 00:04:36.950
family and how he must continue their traditions,
like it or not; which essentially means that

00:04:36.950 --> 00:04:39.350
he has to make shoes rather than music.

00:04:39.350 --> 00:04:43.220
And to emphasise that point, at the end of
the song, Miguel's grandmother swipes his

00:04:43.220 --> 00:04:44.560
guitar away from him.

00:04:44.560 --> 00:04:48.870
Now, I'm not a huge fan of this particular
song, but it gets across the idea of family

00:04:48.870 --> 00:04:50.920
obligation that it was going for.

00:04:50.920 --> 00:04:55.500
However, it does seem rather odd to have the
Riveras break into song to express themselves

00:04:55.500 --> 00:05:00.930
when a major plot point of the movie is that
the Rivera family absolutely hates music!

00:05:00.930 --> 00:05:04.880
And that's exactly what the creators of Coco
realised wasn't working about the full-on

00:05:04.880 --> 00:05:06.400
musical version of the film.

00:05:06.400 --> 00:05:07.400
"Rivera!

00:05:07.400 --> 00:05:08.400
Rivera!

00:05:08.400 --> 00:05:09.400
Rivera!"

00:05:09.400 --> 00:05:10.400
"We do what we do because it is the good to
the beautiful life that we got!"

00:05:10.400 --> 00:05:11.400
"Rivera!

00:05:11.400 --> 00:05:12.400
Rivera!

00:05:12.400 --> 00:05:13.400
Rivera!"

00:05:13.400 --> 00:05:14.400
"Delicioso!"

00:05:14.400 --> 00:05:25.120
"Riveras are thethered forever and ever and
ever and ever and whether we like it or not!"

00:05:25.120 --> 00:05:28.330
"Thank you abuelita!

00:05:28.330 --> 00:05:30.060
That was very educational."

00:05:30.060 --> 00:05:34.500
"Now can I borrow your workbench?"

00:05:34.500 --> 00:05:38.040
While the 'Way of the Riveras' tune didn't
make it into the final film, the movie does

00:05:38.040 --> 00:05:42.470
include a scene that gets the same point across
by showing Miguel's relatives telling him

00:05:42.470 --> 00:05:46.230
that from now on, after his school day has
finished, he'll join them working in their

00:05:46.230 --> 00:05:50.320
family shoemaking business, because as they
tell him: 'You are a Rivera.

00:05:50.320 --> 00:05:52.830
And a Rivera is a shoemaker.

00:05:52.830 --> 00:05:53.940
Through and Through.'

00:05:53.940 --> 00:05:58.810
However, the music wasn't the only major change
that Coco underwent during its six years of

00:05:58.810 --> 00:06:00.270
production.

00:06:00.270 --> 00:06:04.311
Director Lee Unkrich's original idea for Coco
was to tell the tale of a young boy in the

00:06:04.311 --> 00:06:08.300
United States whose father was American and
whose mother was Mexican.

00:06:08.300 --> 00:06:12.060
In the script for that initial story, the
boy visits Mexico for the Day of the Dead

00:06:12.060 --> 00:06:16.130
and, as he faces his grief for his mother
who's passed away, he eventually learns to

00:06:16.130 --> 00:06:18.260
move on and let go of her.

00:06:18.260 --> 00:06:21.680
Of course, approaching the story in this way
would have meant that Coco would have been

00:06:21.680 --> 00:06:25.880
told from an entirely different perspective,
specifically an American one.

00:06:25.880 --> 00:06:29.370
What really changed the filmmakers' minds
was that, after several research trips to

00:06:29.370 --> 00:06:33.620
Mexico and everything they discovered about
Día de Muertos, they realised that the story

00:06:33.620 --> 00:06:37.420
they were planning to tell about grief and
'letting go of a loved one who's died' was

00:06:37.420 --> 00:06:40.310
not what the Day of the Dead was actually
about.

00:06:40.310 --> 00:06:45.150
Instead, Unkrich said he learned that Día
de Muertos, 'isn't about grieving; it's a

00:06:45.150 --> 00:06:46.150
celebration.

00:06:46.150 --> 00:06:49.190
It's about remembering those family members
and loved ones who've passed, and keeping

00:06:49.190 --> 00:06:50.250
them close.'

00:06:50.250 --> 00:06:54.810
So, the team behind Coco decided to ditch
their initial story idea, and focus instead

00:06:54.810 --> 00:06:59.110
on the themes of family and music, setting
the movie entirely in Mexico.

00:06:59.110 --> 00:07:03.639
Now, would you have liked to see Coco as a
full-on musical movie?

